+# 2026-11-11 Does Node 22 global fetch() honour HTTPS_PROXY by default?
+
+## Answer
+
+**No.** In Node 22, global `fetch()` ignores `HTTPS_PROXY`, `HTTP_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` by
+default. It uses them only if you turn on one of these opt-ins:
+
+- the CLI flag `--use-env-proxy`, or
+- the environment variable `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`.
+
+With either one set, Node installs undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent` as the global dispatcher. Global
+`fetch()` then sends both `http:` and `https:` requests through the proxy. The proxy is reached
+with a `CONNECT` tunnel.
+
+If the Node 22 build is too old to have these opt-ins, use the older workaround. Install undici
+from npm and call `setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent())` from the `undici` package. The
+`undici` package is the userland copy, separate from the one built into Node.
+
+## Evidence (all checked against the installed Node v22.23.4)
+
+### 1. Node source: `lib/internal/process/pre_execution.js`, `setupHttpProxy()`
+
+This source was taken from the JS bundled into `/opt/node22/bin/node` v22.23.4:
+
+```js
+function setupHttpProxy() {
+  // This normalized from both --use-env-proxy and NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY settings.
+  if (!getOptionValue('--use-env-proxy')) {
+    return;
+  }
+  if (!process.env.HTTP_PROXY && !process.env.HTTPS_PROXY &&
+      !process.env.http_proxy && !process.env.https_proxy) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  const { setGlobalDispatcher, EnvHttpProxyAgent } = require('internal/deps/undici/undici');
+  const envHttpProxyAgent = new EnvHttpProxyAgent();
+  setGlobalDispatcher(envHttpProxyAgent);
+  // For fetch, we need to set the global dispatcher from here.
+  // ...
+  // TODO(joyeecheung): This is currently guarded with NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY and --use-env-proxy.
+  // Investigate whether it's possible to enable it by default without stepping on other
+  // existing libraries that sets the global dispatcher or monkey patches the global agent.
+}
+```
+
+The function returns early unless the opt-in is set. The TODO comment says outright that the
+behaviour is not enabled by default.
+
+### 2. Node's built-in help (`node --help`, v22.23.4)
+
+```
+--use-env-proxy   parse proxy settings from HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY/NO_PROXY environment
+                  variables and apply the setting in global HTTP/HTTPS clients
+```
+
+### 3. Test run
+
+The test script is at `/tmp/claude-0/-tmp-hs-bench-hNoAzY/00d45ce1-9c83-4792-a266-62a5e4ab52e4/scratchpad/run.js`.
+It starts a local HTTP proxy and sets `HTTP_PROXY` and `HTTPS_PROXY` to point at it. It then runs
+`fetch()` in a child Node process against a host that doesn't exist:
+
+| Mode | URL | Result | Proxy saw |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| default (proxy vars set only) | http://example.invalid/ | `ENOTFOUND` (direct DNS lookup) | nothing |
+| `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` | http://example.invalid/ | proxy error | `CONNECT example.invalid:80` |
+| `--use-env-proxy` | http://example.invalid/ | proxy error | `CONNECT example.invalid:80` |
+| default (proxy vars set only) | https://example.invalid/ | `ENOTFOUND` (direct DNS lookup) | nothing |
+| `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` | https://example.invalid/ | proxy error | `CONNECT example.invalid:443` |
+| `--use-env-proxy` | https://example.invalid/ | proxy error | `CONNECT example.invalid:443` |
+
+By default, `fetch()` connects directly. The proxy is used only with an opt-in.
+
+## Caveats and open items
+
+- **Which 22.x version added the opt-ins: not checked.** Web access wasn't available in this
+  session, so I couldn't check the `added:` lines in `doc/api/cli.md` and `doc/api/http.md` on the
+  v22.x branch. From memory, `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` arrived in v24.0.0 and `--use-env-proxy` in
+  v24.5.0, and both were backported to the 22.x line around v22.21.0. Confirm this before relying
+  on it. Earlier 22.x releases have no built-in opt-in at all.
+- The opt-in is read once, at startup. Setting `process.env.HTTPS_PROXY` from inside a running
+  script does not turn it on.
+- Explicitly setting your own global dispatcher, or passing a `dispatcher` to `fetch`, overrides
+  the proxy agent.
+- To check: <https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.x/doc/api/cli.md> (`NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY`,
+  `--use-env-proxy`) and <https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/v22.x/doc/api/http.md> (the built-in
+  proxy support section).
